// The MoonBit backend: a script block as a dispatch function over the typed
// state struct.
//
// The second of three backends over one AST, and the one whose existence is
// the argument for the corpus. The interpreter walks `@tutuca.Value` where
// every number is a `Num(Double)`; this walks a MoonBit struct where `.count`
// is an `Int` and `.history` an `Array[Int]`. Two implementations of one
// semantics, and the only thing that keeps them honest is being held to the
// same table.
//
// The numeric rule is what makes them agree by construction: **every
// arithmetic expression is emitted as `Double`**, and the conversion happens
// where a value meets a declared type. That is not a convenience — it is the
// interpreter's own model (`Value` has a single `Num(Double)`) written out in
// a language that has more than one number, so a card and a compiled component
// round the same expression the same way.
//
// What it cannot compile it REFUSES, by name, with the reason. The design
// reserves roughly 15% of the corpus for MoonBit — `walk_path`, request
// options, node-replacing `swap`, dyncomp lifecycle — and a backend that
// guessed at those would be worse than one that says which handler to write by
// hand.
import {
  // The `Leg` an `SEffect` carries, and nothing else — this package emits the
  // TEXT of a call into `core` rather than making one.
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/core" @tutuca,
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/tscript",
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/tscript/check",
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/statedef",
}
